{"maxId":74,"entries":{"chapter-52-study-osteoarthritis|Chapter 52 Study: Osteoarthritis":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|~240 million worldwide have symptomatic OA; ≥32 million in the US. OA is the 3rd leading cause of years lived with disability in the US and the most common cause of mobility limitation in older adults (p779).":["u3"],"copy|1 in 2 lifetime risk of developing symptomatic knee OA (p779).":["u4"],"copy|Hip or knee OA carries 20% excess mortality vs those without it (p779).":["u5"],"copy|Radiographic OA (adults >60): hand 20%–40%, knee 30%–40%. Symptomatic OA: hand 13%–26%, knee 10%–14%, hip (≥45) ~10%, foot (>50) 17% — disabling in 9.5% (p779).":["u6"],"copy|Economic burden: ~$65 billion lost wages, >$100 billion medical costs annually in the US (p779).":["u7"],"copy|Obesity is the strongest modifiable risk factor; reducing weight from obese to normal is estimated to cut knee OA incidence by 21% (men) / 33% (women) (p780).":["u8"],"copy|Genetics: 90 genome-wide significant OA risk loci identified; COL2A1 (type II collagen) is among the implicated genes (p780).":["u9"],"copy|Table 52-1 — Secondary causes of OA: anatomical abnormalities, metabolic/endocrine (diabetes mellitus, hemochromatosis, acromegaly, hyperparathyroidism, ochronosis), crystal deposition disease (gout, pseudogout), inflammatory arthritis, trauma, and others (avascular necrosis, neuropathic Charcot joints) (p780).":["u10"],"copy|OA is diagnosed clinically; radiographs are not required to make the diagnosis (p782).":["u11"],"copy|ACR classification criteria: age >50, <30 min morning stiffness, crepitus, bony enlargement/tenderness, normal inflammatory labs, radiographic osteophytes — for classification, not required for diagnosis (p781).":["u12"],"copy|Synovial fluid WBC <2000 cells/mm³ is consistent with OA; >2000 cells/mm³ suggests inflammatory or infectious arthritis (p783).":["u13"],"copy|OA-related lumbar spinal stenosis: symptoms relieved by forward bending (walking uphill, leaning on a shopping cart), worsened by backward bending (p782).":["u14"],"copy|A physical therapy regimen (strengthening + neuromuscular training) improves symptoms in two-thirds of patients with advanced knee OA (p784).":["u15"],"copy|Cane: use in the hand opposite the affected hip/knee; fit to the level of the greater trochanter; elbow bend ~15°–20° when walking (p787).":["u16"],"copy|Weight-loss target: >5% loss achieved within a 20-week period (p786).":["u17"],"copy|Heat and cold (cryotherapy): each limited to 20-minute intervals (p787).":["u18"],"copy|Topical NSAIDs are first-line, before oral NSAIDs, for hand/knee/foot OA; not recommended for hip OA (joint depth precludes efficacy) (p787).":["u19"],"copy|Topical capsaicin: 0.03% is better tolerated than 0.08%; no demonstrated benefit in hip OA (p787).":["u20"],"copy|Acetaminophen: the ACR/AF OA guideline uses a conservative 3 g/day maximum for regular treatment, with monitoring and lower limits when indicated. The FDA general adult ceiling across all products is 4 g in 24 hours. Follow the lower product- or patient-specific limit, especially with liver disease, low body weight or substantial alcohol exposure (p787; FDA, ACR/AF OA guideline).":["u21"],"copy|Avoid NSAIDs if CKD stage IV or V (eGFR <30 cc/min) (p788).":["u22"],"copy|ACR/AF and OARSI recommend against glucosamine or chondroitin sulfate for knee/hip OA (p788). Bisphosphonates showed no efficacy for pain or function, and TNF inhibitors/IL-1 receptor antagonists were not efficacious for erosive OA and are not recommended (p788); the ACR/AF figure lists all three as strongly recommended against (Figure 52-5, p786).":["u23"],"copy|Intra-articular glucocorticoids may relieve pain for up to 2 months. Hazzard discourages more than three injections in six months (p789). Repeat injections are individualized. EULAR describes a customary limit of 3–4 per joint per year, while acknowledging limited evidence for a precise safe frequency (EULAR intra-articular therapy recommendations, 2021).":["u24"],"copy|Viscosupplementation (IA hyaluronic acid): not recommended by ACR/AF/OARSI (p789). Platelet-rich plasma/mesenchymal stem cell therapy: still experimental and not FDA-approved — only within an FDA-approved trial (p789); ACR/AF strongly against for knee/hip (Figure 52-5, p786).":["u25"],"copy|High tibial osteotomy: ~25% failure rate at 10 years (p789).":["u26"],"copy|Total joint replacement: 20%–30% of patients not satisfied post-op; max improvement at 3–6 months, plateauing by 9–12 months; implants typically last ≥15 years (p789).":["u27"],"copy|First CMC (thumb base) OA → trapeziectomy is the procedure of choice (p790).":["u28"],"copy|Prevention: ≥150 min moderate-intensity or ≥75 min vigorous-intensity aerobic activity per week (Physical Activity Guidelines for Americans) (p790).":["u29"],"recall|Recall":["u30"],"copy|CHAPTER 52s / HAZZARD":["u31"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 52; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u32"],"copy|Epidemiology & burden":["u33"],"copy|Risk factors":["u34"],"copy|Diagnosis":["u35"],"copy|Nonpharmacologic management":["u36"],"copy|Pharmacologic management":["u37"],"copy|Surgery & prevention":["u38"],"copy|1.":["u39"],"copy|How many people in the US have symptomatic OA, and what is its rank among causes of disability?":["u40"],"copy|At least 32 million people (out of ~240 million worldwide with symptomatic OA); OA is the 3rd leading cause of years lived with disability in the US (p779).":["u41"],"copy|3.":["u42"],"copy|By how much is weight loss from obese to normal weight estimated to reduce knee OA incidence, in men vs. women?":["u43"],"copy|An estimated 21% in men and 33% in women (p780).":["u44"],"copy|5.":["u45"],"copy|What ACR criteria support classifying a patient's joint pain as OA?":["u46"],"copy|Age >50 years, <30 minutes of morning stiffness, crepitus, bony enlargement/tenderness on exam, normal inflammatory labs, OA-consistent synovial fluid and negative rheumatoid factor, and radiographic osteophytes (p781).":["u47"],"copy|7.":["u48"],"copy|How does bending direction affect OA-related lumbar spinal stenosis symptoms?":["u49"],"copy|Symptoms are relieved by bending slightly forward (eg, walking uphill or leaning on a shopping cart) and worsened by bending backward (eg, walking downhill) (p782).":["u50"],"copy|9.":["u51"],"copy|Which hand should hold a cane, and what elbow angle is targeted?":["u52"],"copy|The hand opposite the affected hip or knee; the elbow should bend approximately 15 to 20 degrees when walking with the cane (p787).":["u53"],"copy|10.":["u54"],"copy|What weight-loss target and timeframe are recommended for OA management?":["u55"],"copy|More than 5% weight loss achieved within a 20-week period (p786).":["u56"],"copy|11.":["u57"],"copy|Which OA drug therapies does the ACR/AF guideline recommend against, and why?":["u58"],"copy|Glucosamine and chondroitin sulfate (no more effective than placebo in the lowest-bias data), TNF inhibitors and IL-1 receptor antagonists (not efficacious for erosive OA), and bisphosphonates (no efficacy for pain or function) (p788; Figure 52-5, p786).":["u59"],"copy|14.":["u60"],"copy|How many intra-articular glucocorticoid injections are recommended within a 6-month period, and roughly how long does the pain relief last?":["u61"],"copy|Hazzard advises against more than three injections in six months and describes pain relief lasting up to two months (p789).":["u62"],"copy|EULAR cites a customary limit of 3–4 injections in the same joint per year, with limited evidence for a universal safe count.":["u62"],"copy|15.":["u63"],"copy|What is the 10-year failure rate of high tibial osteotomy, and what is the surgical procedure of choice for first CMC (thumb base) OA?":["u64"],"copy|Approximately 25% failure rate at 10 years for high tibial osteotomy; trapeziectomy is the procedure of choice for first CMC joint OA (p789–790).":["u65"],"copy|Answer":["u66","u67","u68","u69","u70","u71","u72","u73","u74"],"copy|The chapter estimates ~240 million people worldwide with symptomatic OA and ≥32 million in the US. In the data it cites, OA ranked third for years lived with disability in the US and was the most common cause of mobility limitation in older adults (p779).":["u3"],"copy|Genetics: 90 genome-wide significant OA risk loci reported in the studies available to the chapter; COL2A1 (type II collagen) is among the implicated genes (p780).":["u9"],"copy|Viscosupplementation (intra-articular hyaluronic acid) has little clear benefit in low-bias trials. ACR/AF recommends against it for knee OA, while OARSI allows selected knee use depending on comorbidities (p789 and the guideline comparison above). Hazzard advises restricting PRP and mesenchymal stem-cell treatments to trials (p789). ACR/AF recommends against both for knee and hip OA. For symptomatic knee OA specifically, AAOS gives limited support to PRP for pain and function because trial results vary. These treatments have not been shown to restore lost cartilage. AAOS knee OA guideline, Platelet-rich Plasma.":["u25"],"copy|The chapter reports at least 32 million people in the US and about 240 million worldwide.":["u41"],"copy|OA ranked third for years lived with disability in the US estimates it cites (p779).":["u41"],"copy|Glucosamine for hand, hip or knee OA, and chondroitin sulfate for hip or knee OA.":["u59"],"copy|Hand OA is an exception: ACR/AF conditionally recommends chondroitin sulfate for this site.":["u59"],"copy|Other drugs recommended against include TNF inhibitors and IL-1 receptor antagonists (not efficacious for erosive OA), and bisphosphonates (no efficacy for pain or function) (p788; Figure 52-5, p786).":["u59"]}}
